1
00:00:04,161 --> 00:00:06,871
Welcome to MutualAid-Agent, a serverless

2
00:00:06,871 --> 00:00:12,154
autonomous AI coordinator built with the Strands Agents SDK and Amazon Bedrock

3
00:00:12,154 --> 00:00:14,593
for the Agents for Humans Hackathon.

4
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:19,538
When severe weather strikes—such as sudden flash floods, winter blizzards

5
00:00:19,538 --> 00:00:24,822
or violent windstorms—municipal emergency services are frequently overwhelmed.

6
00:00:24,822 --> 00:00:26,718
In almost every neighborhood

7
00:00:26,718 --> 00:00:32,137
the tools needed to prevent catastrophic damage already exist: submersible pumps

8
00:00:32,137 --> 00:00:35,050
backup generators, chainsaws, and sandbags.

9
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:38,505
But finding them in a panic causes critical delays.

10
00:00:43,514 --> 00:00:46,797
Built specifically for the Good Neighbor Agents track

11
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:50,389
MutualAid-Agent operates on a core philosophy: in a crisis

12
00:00:50,389 --> 00:00:52,123
the best agent is invisible.

13
00:00:52,123 --> 00:00:56,707
Community organizers and mutual aid volunteers do not need another complex

14
00:00:56,707 --> 00:00:58,813
dashboard to monitor or configure.

15
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:03,644
They need an autonomous background agent that quietly does the heavy data work

16
00:01:03,644 --> 00:01:07,298
and surfaces only when a single human decision is required.

17
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:13,253
Under the hood

18
00:01:13,253 --> 00:01:18,105
MutualAid-Agent uses AWS Lambda handlers to ingest real-time storm radar

19
00:01:18,105 --> 00:01:20,665
webhooks and community distress pings.

20
00:01:20,665 --> 00:01:26,123
The Strands Agents SDK coordinates the agentic reasoning loop with Amazon Bedrock

21
00:01:26,123 --> 00:01:31,243
while Amazon DynamoDB serves as our ultra-low latency community resource and

22
00:01:31,243 --> 00:01:32,254
state registry.

23
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:41,622
Let us inspect the live community inventory registered in DynamoDB.

24
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:46,961
Here we see verified neighborhood assets: Neighbor Bob's electric submersible pump

25
00:01:46,961 --> 00:01:52,039
Dave's high-flow gas pump, Sarah's seven-kilowatt generator, Carlos's chainsaw

26
00:01:52,039 --> 00:01:55,164
and pre-filled sandbags at the community center.

27
00:01:59,284 --> 00:02:02,151
Now, let us simulate a live disaster event.

28
00:02:02,151 --> 00:02:07,351
A severe Flash Flood Warning webhook arrives from the National Weather Service

29
00:02:07,351 --> 00:02:11,884
reporting rapid basement and driveway inundation at ten Main Street.

30
00:02:15,939 --> 00:02:20,395
Instantly, the Strands Agent springs into action in the background.

31
00:02:20,395 --> 00:02:21,725
In under two seconds

32
00:02:21,725 --> 00:02:25,783
Amazon Bedrock parses the unstructured meteorological warning

33
00:02:25,783 --> 00:02:29,507
categorizes the required equipment as a submersible pump

34
00:02:29,507 --> 00:02:33,764
and runs a geospatial Haversine proximity query across DynamoDB.

35
00:02:33,764 --> 00:02:38,619
It matches Neighbor Bob's pump, located just point zero three miles away.

36
00:02:43,684 --> 00:02:47,763
Observe the human coordinator's phone simulator on the right.

37
00:02:47,763 --> 00:02:53,515
Rather than bombarding the coordinator with raw telemetry or multiple-choice dropdowns

38
00:02:53,515 --> 00:02:58,597
MutualAid-Agent synthesizes the situation into a single-decision SMS prompt:

39
00:02:58,597 --> 00:03:00,269
Flood at ten Main Street.

40
00:03:00,269 --> 00:03:04,750
Dispatch Neighbor Bob's two-inch pump, point zero three miles away?

41
00:03:04,750 --> 00:03:07,492
Reply YES to approve, NO for alternative.

42
00:03:12,505 --> 00:03:14,988
The coordinator simply replies YES.

43
00:03:14,988 --> 00:03:20,521
Watch what happens immediately upon receiving this single binary confirmation.

44
00:03:25,254 --> 00:03:30,312
The inbound Twilio webhook triggers the agent to mark the proposal APPROVED and

45
00:03:30,312 --> 00:03:33,642
updates Bob's pump status to DISPATCHED in DynamoDB.

46
00:03:33,642 --> 00:03:34,539
Simultaneously

47
00:03:34,539 --> 00:03:39,533
the agent automatically texts staging instructions and the destination address

48
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:41,646
directly to Neighbor Bob's phone.

49
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:49,125
What if Neighbor Bob is unavailable?

50
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:54,328
If the coordinator replies NO, the agent does not fail or drop the incident.

51
00:03:54,328 --> 00:03:59,668
It immediately queries DynamoDB for the next closest candidate—Neighbor Dave's

52
00:03:59,668 --> 00:04:04,324
three-inch gas pump—and drafts a new single-decision SMS in seconds.

53
00:04:09,342 --> 00:04:13,005
MutualAid-Agent adapts across diverse disaster categories.

54
00:04:13,005 --> 00:04:17,868
For a severe windstorm with a downed tree blocking the road at five Pine Road

55
00:04:17,868 --> 00:04:22,857
the agent automatically classifies the need for a chainsaw and matches Neighbor

56
00:04:22,857 --> 00:04:24,246
Carlos within seconds.

57
00:04:29,251 --> 00:04:34,570
MutualAid-Agent demonstrates how autonomous background AI built with the Strands

58
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:38,891
Agents SDK and Amazon Bedrock can transform community resilience.

59
00:04:38,891 --> 00:04:45,473
By handling repetitive emergency logistics in the background and surfacing only for human approvals

60
00:04:45,473 --> 00:04:49,994
we save homes, protect lives, and empower good neighbors everywhere.

61
00:04:49,994 --> 00:04:50,659
Thank you.
